BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jYfWUZQxvU_mD-6c28632
SUMMARY:Probabilistic Analysis of Nonstationary Hydroclimate Extremes
DTSTAMP:20260709T045828Z
DTSTART:20260715T150000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Yuchen Lu  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis Defense
	  Department: Civil and Environmental Engineering  Location: Ryon Engineer
	ing Building 112  Hydroclimate extremes\, including extreme rainfall and t
	ropical cyclones\, pose substantial threats to communities\, infrastructur
	e systems\, and ecosystems. Probabilistic characterization of these extrem
	es is essential for risk assessment\, infrastructure design\, mitigation p
	lanning\, and climate adaptation. Many commonly used approaches for estima
	ting hydroclimate hazards assume stationarity\, even though observations a
	nd climate projections show that extreme events are influenced by climate 
	variability and long-term climate change. However\, accounting for this no
	nstationarity poses methodological challenges: observations of rare events
	 are sparse in time and space\, while climate model products contain syste
	matic biases. To address these challenges\, this dissertation develops sta
	tistical frameworks that incorporate nonstationarity into probabilistic an
	alyses of hydroclimate extremes using limited observational records.  &#16
	0\;  The first chapter develops a hierarchical Bayesian space-time framewo
	rk for nonstationary analysis of extreme rainfall probabilities. This fram
	ework mitigates the challenge of larger sampling variability and uncertain
	ty by incorporating time-varying climate covariates and spatial pooling\, 
	allowing information to be shared across stations while estimating spatial
	ly coherent changes in daily extreme rainfall.  The second chapter extends
	 this analysis to multi-duration rainfall frequency estimates by incorpora
	ting duration dependence. This work addresses the challenge of maintaining
	 coherence across both space and durations\, which is especially important
	 for short-duration rainfall extremes where observations are particularly 
	sparse.  The third chapter develops a nonstationary joint probability fram
	ework for characterizing tropical cyclone parameters relevant to hazard an
	alysis. This approach mitigates the challenge of limited local tropical cy
	clone observations by using regional changes in storm characteristics to i
	nform local-scale analyses and by linking dependent storm parameters throu
	gh physically motivated relationships.  Together\, these three studies add
	ress a common problem in hydroclimate risk analysis: how to estimate chang
	ing extremes from limited and spatially heterogeneous observations. Across
	 rainfall and tropical cyclone applications\, the proposed frameworks impr
	ove the stability\, spatial coherence\, and physical interpretability of n
	onstationary hazard estimates. These methods provide a basis for more robu
	st infrastructure design and climate adaptation planning under changing hy
	droclimate risk.  (Department : Civil and Environmental Engineering)\n\nht
	tps://events.rice.edu/live/events/433735-probabilistic-analysis-of-nonstat
	ionary\n\nFrom Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/433735-probabilistic-analysis-of-no
	nstationary
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Ryon Engineering Building
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:xF91GEqc2YOaDz6csdc3m
SUMMARY:Proton-Conductive Porous Solid Electrolytes for CO2 Electrolysis in
	to Pure Liquid Products
DTSTAMP:20260709T045828Z
DTSTART:20260715T153000Z
DESCRIPTION:&#160\;  Speaker: Abdullah Alazmi  Doctoral Candidate  Thesis D
	efense  Department: Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering  Location: Ralph
	 O'Connor Building 5th floor  The carbon dioxide reduction reaction (CO2RR
	) offers a promising route for converting CO2 into valuable chemical feeds
	tocks\, addressing both environmental and industrial needs. Achieving CO2R
	R electrolyzer devices that are highly efficient requires not only selecti
	ve and stable catalysts for the reduction reactions\, but also optimal dev
	ice engineering\, which includes ion transport between the cathode and ano
	de sides of the electrochemical cell mediated by electrolytes. Despite the
	 decreasing cost of renewable energy improving the viability of electroche
	mically produced feedstocks\, the liquid fuels and chemicals produced thro
	ugh CO2RR are of low purity due to contamination from aqueous electrolytes
	 collected with the products\, increasing the overall cost of the products
	.  This dissertation presents new classes of solid electrolytes that enabl
	e the operation of CO2RR reactors without the use of aqueous electrolytes\
	, thereby minimizing the overall product cost by producing pure liquid pro
	ducts. This approach enables water to flow through the solid electrolyte l
	ayer while also transporting ions\, mediated by the proton-conductive surf
	ace functionality.  To address the broader challenge of solid electrolyte 
	design\, we investigated multiple material platforms\, including mesoporou
	s silica\, electrospun polymers\, and silica-polymer composite membranes t
	o establish relationships between structure\, porosity\, and ionic conduct
	ivity. Among these materials\, we demonstrate strong performance\, achievi
	ng a high proton conductivity of 7.2 x 10-2 S cm-1 through sulfonated sili
	ca particles\, significantly exceeding commercial solid electrolyte resins
	. These results highlight the effectiveness of nanoscale surface functiona
	lization in enabling efficient proton transport while maintaining fluid pa
	thways. This work establishes design principles for porous solid electroly
	tes and identifies promising platforms for enabling high-efficiency\, cont
	aminant-free CO2 electrolysis.  &#160\;  https://riceuniversity.zoom.us/j/
	6699460372?pwd=geShPhx3vChkVLk9HhWkOjMrgg5VW7.1&amp\;omn=97998132641  (Dep
	artment : Chemical and Biomolecular Engineering)\n\nhttps://events.rice.ed
	u/live/events/433803-proton-conductive-porous-solid-electrolytes-for\n\nFr
	om Rice University - Open to the Public
URL:https://events.rice.edu/live/events/433803-proton-conductive-porous-sol
	id-electrolytes-for
GEO:29.7174;-95.4018
LOCATION:Ralph O'Connor Building
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:rice-public-events
CATEGORIES:Rice University - Open to the Public
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT1H
END:VEVENT
